\section{The treatment's type}{
The treatment decides the model for the propensity score and what that score
even is.\tabular{lll}{
   treatment \tab propensity score \tab model \cr
   binary \tab one column, the probability of treatment \tab \code{\link[=binomial]{binomial()}} \cr
   \code{K} categories \tab the whole vector of assignment probabilities \tab \code{\link[=multinomial]{multinomial()}} \cr
   continuous \tab a conditional density, not a regression \tab not fitted \cr
}


For a treatment with more than two categories the balancing score is the
\emph{vector} of assignment probabilities (Imbens, 2000; Imai and van Dyk, 2004),
not any one of them, so all of them go into the control function. They sum to
one and are therefore collinear, which costs a tree ensemble nothing.

For a continuous treatment the analogue is the conditional density of the
treatment given the covariates evaluated at the observed dose (Hirano and
Imbens, 2004), which needs a density model rather than a regression, so
\code{propensity = TRUE} is refused and a score supplied as a number is used as
given.
}

\section{The assumption a continuous treatment carries}{
With a continuous treatment this fits \code{f0(x) + z * f1(x)}: a dose response
that is \strong{linear in the dose}, with a slope that varies. For a binary
treatment that is no assumption at all. For a continuous one it is a real one,
and it is the assumption Woody et al. (2020) make and diagnose. If the dose
response itself might be curved, either put the treatment in as an ordinary
predictor or give \code{f1} the treatment among its moderators, which makes the
effect vary across the dose; see \code{\link[=vc]{vc()}}.
}

